  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-qftx-finite-heavy-gaussian-square": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, let $K\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be symmetric positive definite, and let $j\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Let $\\gamma_{K^{-1}}=N_d(0,K^{-1})$, where covariance is the matrix $\\mathbb E[XX^T]$. Then\n$$\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\exp(-\\tfrac12x^TKx+j^Tx)\\,dx} {\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\exp(-\\tfrac12x^TKx)\\,dx} =\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}e^{j^Tx}\\,d\\gamma_{K^{-1}}(x) =\\exp\\!\\left(\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j\\right).$$\nEquivalently, in the finite real Gaussian regulator with normalized weight $e^{-S}$ and $\\hbar=1$, completing the source shift $\\chi=K^{-1}j+\\eta$ gives\n$$\\tfrac12\\chi^TK\\chi-j^T\\chi=\\tfrac12\\eta^TK\\eta-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j.$$\nUse $\\hbar=1$ and coordinate/source units for which $x^TKx$ and $j^Tx$ are dimensionless. Both integrals are finite; their common Gaussian normalization cancels in the ratio. No infinite-dimensional Lebesgue measure or determinant is asserted."
    ],
    "def-qftx-heavy-gaussian-data": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nAssume AC. Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), let $A:D(A)\\subseteq H\\to H$ be densely defined, self-adjoint and nonnegative, and let $M>0$. Set $K=M^2I+A$ on $D(A)$ and let $E_A$ be the spectral measure supplied by [[thm-qftx-positive-unbounded-spectral-calculus]]. Define $f_M(a)=(M^2+a)^{-1}$ for $a\\ge0$. The bounded spectral operator $f_M(A)$ is the inverse $K^{-1}$ and satisfies $\\|K^{-1}\\|\\le M^{-2}$. For $J\\in H$ define the matched quadratic effective-action contribution\n$$\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{eff}}(J):=-\\tfrac12\\langle J,K^{-1}J\\rangle.$$\nThe inner product is the complex Hilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], linear in its first variable; the displayed quadratic form is real and nonpositive. In natural units $\\hbar=c=1$, $A$ and $K$ have mass-squared units; normalize the source/pairing so $\\langle J,K^{-1}J\\rangle$ is dimensionless, hence $\\|J\\|^2$ has mass-squared units. In a finite real regulator, integration of $\\exp[-\\tfrac12\\chi^TK\\chi+j^T\\chi]$ divided by the zero-source Gaussian integral yields $\\exp[\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j]$ by [[lem-qftx-finite-heavy-gaussian-square]], so the corresponding effective action changes by $-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j$. Taking $-\\log$ of that normalized integral uses [[def-natural-logarithm]]. Determinants and Lebesgue integration are finite-regulator quantities only; this definition supplies no infinite-dimensional Gaussian measure or determinant."
    ],
    "thm-qftx-heavy-derivative-bound": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the hypotheses and notation of [[def-qftx-heavy-gaussian-data]]. Let $E\\ge0$ be an energy and let $P_E:=E_A([0,E^2])$ be the spectral projection of $A$. For $J=P_EJ\\in H$ and an integer $N\\ge0$, define\n$$S_NJ:=M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N}(-A/M^2)^rJ,$$\nwhich is well-defined since $J$ has bounded spectral support. Put $R_NJ:=K^{-1}J-S_NJ$. Then\n$$\\|R_NJ\\|\\le M^{-2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}\\|J\\|,$$\nand\n$$\\left|\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{eff}}(J)+\\tfrac12\\langle J,S_NJ\\rangle\\right| =\\tfrac12|\\langle J,R_NJ\\rangle| \\le \\frac{\\|J\\|^2}{2M^2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}.$$\nUse natural units $\\hbar=c=1$: $A$ has units of mass squared, $E,M$ have mass units, and $E^2/M^2$ is dimensionless. Under the source normalization of [[def-qftx-heavy-gaussian-data]], $\\|J\\|^2/M^2$ and the action remainder are dimensionless. These are exact conditional bounds for every $E\\ge0$; the truncation is small/useful when $E<M$. No claim is made for unrestricted sources or arbitrary interacting matching."
    ],
    "def-qftx-finite-gaussian-shell-transform": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nAssume AC and let $d\\ge1$. For a real symmetric positive-semidefinite covariance matrix $C$, write $\\gamma_C=N_d(0,C)$, allowing singular $C$. For a nonnegative Borel function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$, define the normalized shell transform\n$$T_Cf(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x+z)\\,d\\gamma_C(z)\\in[0,\\infty].$$\nThis is a Borel function of $x$. If $V:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty)$ is finite-valued and Borel, define\n$$V_C(x):=-\\log\\bigl(T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\bigr).$$\nThen $V_C$ is a finite nonnegative Borel function, and $0<T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\le1$ for every $x$. The covariance convention is $\\mathbb E[ZZ^T]=C$; $x,z,V$ use the same finite-regulator field-coordinate convention, so $C$ has the square units of those coordinates. The measures $\\gamma_C$ are normalized probabilities, with no determinant/vacuum factor in $T_C$. The transform is defined at finite regulator only."
    ],
    "prop-qftx-finite-gaussian-shell-semigroup": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $d\\ge1$, let $C_1,C_2$ be real symmetric positive-semidefinite $d\\times d$ covariance matrices, and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$ be Borel. With $T_C$ as in [[def-qftx-finite-gaussian-shell-transform]],\n$$T_{C_2}(T_{C_1}f)(x)=T_{C_1+C_2}f(x)\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d),$$\nas an equality in $[0,\\infty]$. In particular, for every finite nonnegative Borel potential $V$,\n$$\\exp[-(V_{C_1})_{C_2}(x)]=\\exp[-V_{C_1+C_2}(x)].$$\nThe Gaussian measures are normalized probabilities, so no determinant/vacuum constant appears; changing to unnormalized Gaussian weights requires carrying their normalization factors. This is an exact finite-regulator semigroup identity. Differentiating it to obtain a Polchinski-type flow is not asserted and would require a differentiable covariance path and separate differentiability/integrability hypotheses."
    ],
    "cex-qftx-eft-outside-band": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $H=L^2(\\mathbb R,dp;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int_\\mathbb R u(p)\\overline{v(p)}\\,dp$, let\n$$A=M_{p^2},\\qquad D(A)=\\{u\\in H:p^2u\\in H\\},$$\nand fix $M>0$, $0\\le E<M$, and $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$. This is the no-band case for the estimate in [[thm-qftx-heavy-derivative-bound]]: the polynomial approximation to $(M^2+p^2)^{-1}$ has multiplier remainder\n$$r_N(p^2)=M^{-2}\\frac{(-p^2/M^2)^{N+1}}{1+p^2/M^2}.$$\nLet $r_N(A)$ denote multiplication by $r_N(p^2)$ on its maximal $L^2$ domain. There is a unit vector $u\\in\\bigcap_{r=0}^{N+1}D(A^r)$ for which\n$$\\|r_N(A)u\\|>M^{-2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}.$$\nThus the derivative estimate's low-energy factor cannot hold uniformly on the full momentum spectrum. Here $p,M,E$ have mass units, $dp$ has mass units, $A$ has mass-squared units, and $\\hbar=c=1$. This is a mathematical counterexample to removing the spectral-band hypothesis; it is not a measured physical failure."
    ]
  },
